ippfix (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A page whose entire content is one invocation of a form XObject, used
    exactly once, is now drawn directly on the page instead. Browsers printing
    through Skia wrap a sheet this way and draw it through a scaling matrix;
    it is legal and renders correctly nearly everywhere, and at least one
    printer accepts such a page, marks a sheet, reports the job completed, and
    produces a page that is correct for the first third and then falls apart.
    Nothing else here can see that: the size guard is off unless a device is
    measured to enforce a limit, the raster retry waits for a rejection that
    never comes, and the page-counter check asks whether a sheet was marked,
    which it was. The rewrite moves operators rather than changing them -- the
    same drawing in the same order, against the resources it was written
    against -- and it refuses anything it cannot show to be equivalent: a page
    that draws anything of its own, a form used more than once, a form with a
    matrix of its own, a form with no resources. ?page-forms=keep declines it.
  * What triggers the underlying fault is not known, and the manual says so.
    The transparency group, the even-odd fills, the oversized bounding box,
    the size of the document, every cumulative measure of work, and the length
    of a graphics-state span were each ruled out by printing them. A synthetic
    page matching the real one's operator counts, volume, structure and
    coordinate precision prints perfectly. What is established is that the
    same content fails inside a form and succeeds drawn directly, which is
    narrow, verified on paper, and enough to act on.

 -- gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:45:39 -0700

ippfix (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * macOS now builds a driverless queue instead of picking a generic driver.
    Four separate things had to be true for that and none of them were. The
    queue is advertised with the printer's own urf-supported tokens as URF;
    it is published under the DNS-SD subtype _universal._sub._ipp._tcp, which
    is the record macOS reads to decide a queue speaks AirPrint; the
    certificate names the advertised addresses, so a client that checks can
    connect at all; and the SRV target is a name the client can resolve. Any
    one of them missing is enough for the queue to arrive as a generic
    printer with no colour and no duplex, which the user cannot override.
  * --advertise-hostname now defaults to this system's .local name rather than
    the advertised address. An SRV target is a domain name and the client is
    expected to resolve it; an address literal is not one, and resolving it
    returns nothing. The old default worked only because the address record
    travels in the same packet and clients match the literal back by
    comparison, which nothing obliges them to do -- and it matters more now
    that macOS takes the driverless path, where that name is resolved and the
    queue built from it. Every URI this proxy hands out is still built from
    --advertise, so nothing durable is lost. Pass --advertise-hostname with an
    address to keep the previous behaviour.
  * A reply too large to parse is no longer relayed with the printer's own
    address in it. The proxy exists so that clients never learn where the
    printer is, and the one path that gave up parsing gave that away.
  * A page placed off the sheet by its producer is put back. Some senders
    write a page whose content sits outside the sheet the job asks for, and
    it prints shifted and clipped. The repair triggers only on a shape that
    can be recognised with confidence, verifies it has not made the job
    worse, prints the original if it has, and reports every job it seriously
    considered. It is off unless --repair-placement asks for it.
  * A document the printer accepts and then cannot read is sent again as
    raster. Some printers take a job, report it accepted, and discard it for
    a format they will not admit to refusing. The retry never covers pages
    that already came out: a failure part way through a job is not a reason
    to print its first pages twice. --no-raster-retry declines it.
  * Lossless images are no longer re-encoded as JPEG on the way through the
    converter, which was quietly costing quality on every converted job.
  * The archive empties itself on a clock. Jobs are kept for a month, and
    sooner if the directory passes a hundred jobs or half a gigabyte. The age
    bound is the one that holds when nobody is printing and nobody is
    watching.
  * Job splitting is gone. It worked around a size limit that printers
    declare and do not enforce, and measuring one showed it never applied.
    The converter is asked for a page range and a page count instead.
  * The raster path is told which side of the paper it is imaging, so duplex
    output is no longer imaged as if every sheet were the first.
  * A printer no longer reports a cartridge as empty and low at the same
    time, and supply levels are reported as what they are rather than as if
    they settled anything.
  * Attribute replies far larger than anything expected are parsed rather
    than refused: the limit counted wire records, not attributes, and a
    single ordinary reply from a multifunction device could exceed it.

 -- gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 10 Sep 2026 02:11:57 -0700

ippfix (1.0.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reports now cross-check the printer's job accounting against its own RFC
    3805 page counter over SNMP. job-impressions-completed comes from the
    firmware that just claimed success for a job it did not print; the page
    counter comes from the marking engine. A job the printer says it printed
    while its counter did not move is a failure nothing else here would have
    noticed, and is now reported.
  * The counter is checked before it is believed -- the printer is asked what
    its counter counts -- and switched off, loudly, if it goes backwards twice,
    jumps implausibly, stops answering, or repeatedly fails to move for jobs
    reporting impressions. It never raises an alert of its own until it has
    been seen to move for a job that did print. --no-page-counter, or
    ?page-counter=off per printer.
  * New optional SNMP relay, so the printer's counters are reachable from
    networks the printer is not. Read-only GET and GETNEXT inside the Printer
    MIB; GETBULK, SET, SNMPv3 and everything outside those subtrees are
    refused; rate limited per source and overall. Its socket is shipped
    disabled: opening a UDP port is the administrator's decision.
  * The relay can serve several printers, one per address: ?snmp-relay=ADDRESS
    names the listener a printer owns, and listeners are matched to printers by
    the address they bound rather than by what the units are called. With
    several printers and only a wildcard listener it answers nothing and says
    so, rather than picking one.
  * Per-printer settings now travel as a query string on the printer's URI,
    and an unrecognised one is an error rather than silently ignored.
  * SNMP strings are stripped of the NUL padding printer firmware adds, and
    printer status strings are collapsed to one line before they reach a
    report: an HP M430 answers its console text with an embedded newline, which
    in a mailed report would forge a line of it.
  * snmpmini.py replaces the SNMP implementation that probe-printer.py carried
    privately, so the daemon and the probe read the counter the same way.

 -- gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 09:44:24 -0700

ippfix (1.0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reports about a job that did not print now carry what is needed to
    reproduce it: the job as the client sent it, when --archive is on, and the
    job as this proxy handed it to the printer. --alert-max-attachment bounds
    what a report may carry.
  * Reports also record what the printer said about itself at the time. An
    empty cartridge prints nothing and blames nobody, and that is worth ruling
    out before blaming firmware.
  * DNS-SD publishes the advertised address rather than a .local name, so
    printing no longer depends on multicast DNS reaching this host every time.
    --advertise-hostname restores a name where one is wanted.
  * Alerts were queued but never delivered. sendmail(1) forks a delivery agent
    and returns, and the default KillMode killed that agent as soon as the
    oneshot unit's ExecStart exited -- mid-SMTP, silently. KillMode=process in
    ippfix-alert.service, and a drop-in for dma.service, which flushes its
    queue the same way and loses mail for the same reason.
  * StartLimitIntervalSec was in [Service], where systemd ignores it, so the
    proxy did not in fact retry indefinitely as its own comment claimed.
  * New ippfix-archive-reminder: schedules a one-shot reminder that --archive
    is still on, and deletes itself once it has run.

 -- gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 07:46:30 -0700

